 # Local development
 
+## Phase 12 P12-01 production source proof — 2026-09-28
+
+P12-01 is source/local preflight under [ExecPlan 091](plans/091-production-runtime-and-deployment.md),
+not deployment. Production startup uses `python -m brickvault_api.main` from
+the reviewed installed package after later provisioning. It requires explicit
+`BVA_MODE=production`, `BVA_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1`, `BVA_API_PORT`,
+`BVA_PRODUCTION_ORIGIN=https://<approved-host>`, `BVA_RUNTIME_DATABASE_URL`,
+`BVA_DATABASE_OWNERSHIP_MARKER`, `BVA_PROXY_SHARED_SECRET`,
+`BVA_STATIC_ENABLED=true` and an absolute `BVA_WEB_BUILD_DIR`.
+The database URL is a canonical `postgresql+psycopg` URL with a dedicated
+`brickvault_<name>_runtime` role, explicit `127.0.0.1` port and dedicated
+`brickvault_<name>` database; migration uses the distinct matching `_owner`
+role. Both passwords and the proxy secret are generated 64-character lowercase
+hex values. The marker format is
+`brickvault-appraisal:<32-lowercase-hex>:<32-lowercase-hex>` and must match the
+database comment and actual owner. Dev/TEST database names/roles and remote
+hosts are rejected. This describes inputs; do not put real values in source,
+`.env.local`, a frontend build or a review package.
+
+The intended service receives environment values from an admin-protected local
+file outside the checkout (for example via systemd `EnvironmentFile`) with
+restrictive ownership/mode. A separate protected Caddy setting supplies the
+same proxy secret to `header_up X-BrickVault-Proxy` so it overwrites any client
+value. The Caddy route must retain the exact HTTPS Host when proxying to the
+loopback API. Uvicorn keeps `proxy_headers=False`, and the application ignores
+`X-Forwarded-*`. Neither Caddy nor FastAPI may expose the internal proxy
+header in access logs. The optional provider credential root, if enabled in a
+later reviewed slice, must be an absolute admin-protected server directory;
+provider calls were not used in this proof.
+
+`services/api/.venv/Scripts/python.exe scripts/production_runtime_proof.py`
+performs one bounded proof on the owned local TEST PostgreSQL service. It
+creates recorded, run-specific production-style owner/runtime roles and one
+database, migrates the current graph, provisions only an ephemeral synthetic
+principal, tests the synthetic Caddy-to-HTTP request path, checks runtime DDL
+denial, then removes those exact owned resources and restores the TEST service's
+prior state. Its ignored ledger is under `.local/database/runs`; inspect that
+exact ledger before any manual recovery. No real production DB is contacted.
+The proof checks Host/Origin/proxy refusal, Secure Strict cookies, CSRF,
+readiness, private no-store and safe bytes/logs. It is not a certificate or
+real reverse-proxy acceptance test.
+
+For a later reviewed Android build, set only the exact approved HTTPS origin
+as `BVA_ANDROID_PRODUCTION_ORIGIN` in the local process environment and run
+`pnpm build:android-production`. The command builds the shared Vite app in
+`android-production` mode, verifies the public bytes, copies the matching
+Capacitor config/assets and checks that the TEST API and TEST trust are absent.
+The configured WebView origin is the derived `https://android.<approved-host>`;
+the API target is the real approved origin. The P12-01 run used only the
+synthetic `https://app.example.test` origin and built **no APK**. Production
+signing, normal Android certificate validation and physical operation need
+P12-05 acceptance after actual DNS/HTTPS selection.
+
